# Escalation set: six tasks built to separate the rungs — multi-step, path-
# dependent or constrained-optimisation problems where a wrong intermediate step
# poisons the final number. THIS FILE IS DATA. Answers verified in Python
# (simulation / exhaustive search) before being written.
{"id": "e01_trailing_stop", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A long Nifty futures position is entered at the close of candle 1 at 23,800, with a trailing stop 40 points below: the initial stop is 23,760, and after each subsequent candle CLOSES the stop is raised to (that close - 40) if that is higher than the current stop. The stop in force during a candle is the one set at the previous candle's close, and if a candle's LOW touches or crosses the stop, the position exits at the stop price. Candles 2-6 (open/high/low/close): c2 23800/23860/23795/23850; c3 23850/23900/23840/23890; c4 23890/23920/23855/23860; c5 23860/23880/23845/23870; c6 23870/23930/23865/23925. On which candle does the position exit, at what price, and what is the P&L in points?", "expectation": "Exits on candle 5 at 23850 for +50 points. (Stop after c3 close is 23850; c4's low 23855 does not touch it; c5's low 23845 does.) An answer exiting on c4, or exiting at a different stop level, is wrong."}
{"id": "e02_strangle_select", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "From this Nifty option chain, choose ONE put strike and ONE call strike to sell as a short strangle. Puts (strike: premium): 23400: 38, 23500: 55, 23600: 78, 23700: 110. Calls: 24100: 95, 24200: 66, 24300: 44, 24400: 28. Constraints: the lower breakeven (put strike minus total credit) must be at or below 23,500; the upper breakeven (call strike plus total credit) must be at or above 24,300; total credit must be at least 100. Among combinations satisfying all constraints, pick the one with the HIGHEST total credit. State the two strikes, the total credit, and both breakevens.", "expectation": "Sell the 23700 PE and the 24100 CE for a total credit of 205, with breakevens 23495 and 24305. (23495 <= 23500 and 24305 >= 24300 both hold; no other qualifying pair has more credit.)"}
{"id": "e03_broken_wing", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A broken-wing call butterfly on Nifty, all per unit: buy one 23,800 CE at 150, sell two 24,000 CE at 70 each, buy one 24,300 CE at 25. State (a) the net debit, (b) the maximum profit per unit and the index level where it occurs, (c) BOTH breakeven levels at expiry, and (d) the fixed loss per unit for any expiry above 24,300.", "expectation": "Net debit 35; maximum profit 165 per unit at 24,000; breakevens at 23,835 and 24,165; and a flat loss of 135 per unit above 24,300 (the extra 100 of unprotected short spread width plus the 35 debit)."}
{"id": "e04_trading_days", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "In March 2026, the monthly Nifty expiry is the last Thursday, 26 March, but that day is a trading holiday, so expiry shifts to the previous trading day. Trading days are Monday to Friday excluding that holiday. A position is opened on Tuesday 10 March 2026. State the actual expiry date and weekday, and the total number of trading days from 10 March to 26 March inclusive of both endpoints.", "expectation": "Expiry is Wednesday 25 March 2026, and there are 12 trading days in 10-26 March inclusive (13 weekdays minus the 26 March holiday)."}
{"id": "e05_lot_allocation", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A desk may deploy at most 500,000 rupees of margin across two strategies, at most 15 lots of each. An iron condor uses 15,000 rupees of margin per lot and collects 4,500 rupees of credit per lot. A credit spread uses 24,000 rupees of margin per lot and collects 8,100 rupees of credit per lot. How many lots of each maximise total credit collected, and what is that maximum credit? Whole lots only.", "expectation": "9 iron condors and 15 credit spreads, using 495,000 of margin, for a maximum credit of 162,000 rupees. This split is the unique optimum."}
{"id": "e06_straddle_partial", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A short straddle on Sensex (lot size 20): sell the 80,000 CE at 600 and the 80,000 PE at 550 per unit. The index closes expiry up exactly 0.9% from 80,000. What is the P&L per unit and per lot, in rupees? Give signed numbers.", "expectation": "Index closes at 80,720; the call is 720 in the money, so P&L is +430 per unit and +8,600 per lot of 20. Both numbers positive (a partial move that stays inside the 1,150-point credit)."}
